1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:01,821
Dendroecology.

2
00:00:02,276 --> 00:00:06,816
This explanation accompanies a ninety-minute dendroecology lesson.

3
00:00:07,276 --> 00:00:11,976
Pause to complete the calculation and comparison exercises.

4
00:00:12,779 --> 00:00:16,280
Explain the questions addressed by dendroecology.

5
00:00:16,742 --> 00:00:21,169
Relate growth changes, scars and ages to forest history.

6
00:00:21,623 --> 00:00:25,722
Distinguish observed changes from hypotheses about causes.

7
00:00:26,176 --> 00:00:31,189
Consider not only how growth changes but also which comparisons can help evaluate its

8
00:00:31,202 --> 00:00:33,316
possible causes.

9
00:00:34,119 --> 00:00:38,028
What changes inside a stem when insects eat its leaves?.

10
00:00:38,332 --> 00:00:41,990
Separate a dated change from an inference about its cause.

11
00:00:42,450 --> 00:00:47,904
Investigate forest history using ring widths, anatomy and comparisons.

12
00:00:48,358 --> 00:00:53,477
Losing foliage need not produce an immediate proportional reduction in ring width.

13
00:00:53,937 --> 00:00:57,728
Examine response timing and anatomy as well.

14
00:00:58,531 --> 00:01:01,960
Growth: changes in ring width and their duration.

15
00:01:02,415 --> 00:01:06,487
Injuries: dated scars and subsequent wound closure.

16
00:01:06,941 --> 00:01:11,553
Populations: regeneration, establishment and mortality.

17
00:01:12,007 --> 00:01:16,711
Growth, injuries and population ages provide different information.

18
00:01:17,165 --> 00:01:22,140
Combining them helps evaluate the limitations of any single record.

19
00:01:22,943 --> 00:01:26,766
A canopy gap changes light and competitive conditions.

20
00:01:27,222 --> 00:01:31,643
Responses depend on species, position and prior growth.

21
00:01:32,103 --> 00:01:36,491
Reconstruct stand dynamics from multiple individual histories.

22
00:01:36,945 --> 00:01:41,833
Responses to extra light differ among species and prior growth states.

23
00:01:42,287 --> 00:01:47,104
Consider the surrounding conditions before and after a gap opens.

24
00:01:47,907 --> 00:01:51,500
Suppression: a period of relatively slow growth.

25
00:01:51,962 --> 00:01:55,850
Release: a sustained increase in growth to investigate.

26
00:01:56,304 --> 00:02:00,159
One wide ring alone does not establish canopy opening.

27
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:05,607
A sustained increase can be informative, while a single wide ring does not by itself

28
00:02:05,620 --> 00:02:08,588
establish release from competition.

29
00:02:09,391 --> 00:02:14,201
Consider competition, climate, water, insects and management.

30
00:02:14,656 --> 00:02:18,301
Distinguish immediate changes from delayed responses.

31
00:02:18,762 --> 00:02:23,189
Test a hypothesis with independent observations or records.

32
00:02:23,643 --> 00:02:29,314
Compare climate, thinning, recovery after insect damage and other explanations capable of

33
00:02:29,327 --> 00:02:31,920
producing a similar change.

34
00:02:32,723 --> 00:02:36,336
Consider comparison stands or non-host species.

35
00:02:36,791 --> 00:02:41,613
Record site, species, size, crown position and management.

36
00:02:42,067 --> 00:02:46,377
Avoid selecting only affected trees or present-day survivors.

37
00:02:46,831 --> 00:02:51,936
Include comparison trees or non-host species sharing the climatic setting rather than

38
00:02:51,949 --> 00:02:55,324
sampling only visibly affected trees.
